title: Genesis 16 – Hagar and Ishmael
description: A study in Genesis 16: Sarai gives Hagar to Abram, the birth of Ishmael, and the allegory of the bondwoman and the freewoman in Galatians 4.
output: lessons/genesis/genesis-16-hagar-and-ishmael.html

=== Introduction
--- Hagar and Ishmael [title]
overline: A study in Genesis 16
sub: The son of the bondwoman, born after the flesh.
refs: Genesis 16:1-4; Galatians 4:22-23
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

=== Impatient with God
--- Sarai’s plan
scripture: Genesis 16:1-3 | mark: after Abram had dwelt ten years in the land of Canaan
stat: 86 | Abram’s age when Ishmael was born [[Genesis 16:16]]
stat: 76 | Sarai’s age
stat: 10 | years in Canaan
p: The birth of Ishmael by the bondservant Hagar comes about about ten years after settling in Canaan, time well past God’s promise to Abram of an heir, as man reckons. [[Genesis 15:4]]

--- By any means
scripture: Genesis 16:4-6
p: Impatient with God, lacking faith, desperate for an heir and wanting a child by any means, Sarai convinces Abram to sin. To have what is an illegitimate offspring in place of the promised son.
panel-warn: Another older brother | Who doesn’t receive the inheritance or God’s blessing. Like Cain or Esau. Or the Jews. [[Genesis 4:4-5]] [[Genesis 25:23]]

--- The angel of the Lord found her
heading: The angel of the {Lord} found her
scripture: Genesis 16:7-12 | mark: he will be a wild man; his hand will be against every man, and every man’s hand against him
scripture: Genesis 16:13 | mark: Thou God seest me

=== The bondwoman and the freewoman
--- Which things are an allegory
scripture: Galatians 4:21-24 | mark: the one by a bondmaid, the other by a freewoman; Which things are an allegory
scripture: Galatians 4:25-27

--- Children of promise
scripture: Galatians 4:28-31 | mark: as Isaac was, are the children of promise; Cast out the bondwoman and her son
p: This theme, of the elder of the flesh being rejected for the son of the Spirit, of the promise, exists to teach us the way in which we who are sons of God in faith are the true heirs after Abraham, and those of the flesh who rejected Christ are like Ishmael. Cast out.

--- Cast out
lead: In Genesis 21, in accordance with Sarah’s wishes and the {Lord}’s command, Abraham reluctantly banishes his firstborn, Ishmael, and the boy’s mother, the slave Hagar.
refs: Genesis 21:9-12
panel-warn: Children of the bondwoman | Those under the law, using their own works to justify themselves before God, are cast out.
panel: Children of promise | Those conceived by faith, like Isaac was, the saved believers justified in Christ, receive the inheritance.

=== All works religion
--- Always seeking to undermine the faith
lead: This extends not only to the Jews who developed the Talmudic Judaism that spun out of the Pharisaical line following the death and resurrection of Jesus, but to all works-based false religion.
p: In Galatians, Paul writes warning against those Judaizers who sought to put the foolish Galatians back under bondage. There have always been those seeking to undermine the faith that calls upon the name of the {Lord} for salvation. [[Galatians 5:1-4]]
step: Eden | The fig leaves in the garden. [[Genesis 3:7]]
step: Cain | His vegetables and fruit that he laboured to grow. [[Genesis 4:3]]
step: Today | Catholic sacrament and works, Buddhists and Hindus and Muslims, and of course Jews of every stripe.

--- Ishmael and Islam
p: Ishmael is said to be the father of what would be Islam: that the son of the bondwoman’s promised seed were the Arabs and Mohammed. And their acceptance of Jesus as a prophet, but denying His sacrifice to instead trust in their own works, is a reflection of the sin of Sarah and Abraham with Hagar.
center: It’s an interesting correlation, given us by revelation in God’s word. [[Genesis 17:20]]

=== Close
--- Not of the bondwoman [title]
overline: Genesis 16
sub: but of the free.
point: Impatience is unbelief | Sarai and Abram tried to bring God’s promise about by the flesh.
point: The son of the flesh | Like Cain and Esau, the elder of the flesh is not the heir.

--- Not of the bondwoman, continued
point: Children of promise | We, as Isaac was, are the children of promise, by faith.
point: Cast out the bondwoman | All works religion is bondage, and shall not be heir.

